00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.760 align:start position:0%
the numbers behind this story continue  to be very sobering indeed the number of  deaths reported today was one of the  biggest daily totals of any European  country so far now this chart shows the  total number of reported death and how  they've been rising today as you can see  the total is now just over 7,000 but in  order to chart our progress in  controlling this virus we need to  understand the speed at which these  numbers are rising so have a look at  this in early March the number of deaths  was doubling roughly every two days it  then started to slow and as you can see  there now it's doubling roughly every  four days this helps to explain why this  is the number of deaths reported each  day today's 938 is the biggest total yet  but while those bars are getting bigger  they have started to level off ever so  slightly in recent days now that  leveling is even clearer if we show it  to you in a chart that tracks trends so  look at this this is exactly the same  data just displayed in what we call a  logarithmic chart the bars showed the  deaths and the line shows the expected  growth according to recent trends now  you see there's a little space there  between the line and the bars  it's that space which offers a small  glimmer of hope emits what's still a  very dark picture because it shows that  the reported deaths have ever so  slightly under shot expectations but the  pace of growth isn't the same in all  parts of the UK our analysis shows that  while London's still accounts for the  most deaths the numbers there are now  increasing at a slower pace than  elsewhere this shows the percentage  increase in death by nation and by  English region since last Friday  as you can see London is actually right  down there at the bottom while Scotland  the Northeast and Yorkshire and the  Northwest all grew by well over a  hundred percent the same shifting  balance is clear when you look at the  number of people in hospital beds this  chart was presented at today's press  conference and while the total number in  the UK rose by 5 percent in the last 24  hours  it was up 16% in the East of England you  see that labeled G there it raises  questions  whether the lockdown could be lifted at  different times in different regions but  in reality we're probably some weeks  away from any restrictions coming to an  end
 

